usb: typec: max77705: review fixes for VBUS, events, recovery and the mailbox

From review of the Type-C driver on the Galaxy Tab S8+:

- VBUS could stay on while the port was a sink. PRSWAP_SWAPTOSRC enabled
  it without looking at CCStat, a hard reset as sink never turned it
  off, and CC updates that left CCStat unchanged (or moved to audio or
  debug) never checked it. VBUS is now on only while CCStat is SOURCE:
  every CC update turns it off otherwise, the hard-reset path does too,
  the enable itself refuses outside SOURCE, and the swap-to-source
  enable goes through the same checked path.
- PDMsg and SYSMsg are reports, not levels: the next message overwrites
  PD_STATUS0 and USBC_STATUS2, and the work read them after the fact,
  so bursts were coalesced and a BOOT_WDT/BOOT_POR could be missed. The
  interrupt handler now reads them with the interrupt that announced
  them and queues the event in a small kfifo, which the work drains in
  order. An overflow triggers a resync from levels, and a dropped reset
  report is kept in a flag. As a backstop, the work compares the
  interrupt masks with those read back after hw_init; a CCIC reset puts
  them back to their defaults, so a mismatch re-initializes.
- The recovery cap was permanent: after 8 CCIC resets, over any length of
  uptime, the driver stopped re-initializing and carried on with a
  firmware that had forgotten its settings. Recovery is now rate limited
  (4 per 60 s). Past the limit, or when hw_init fails, the port is held
  safe (VBUS off, charger input limit at 500 mA, events ignored) and
  recovery is tried again once the window has passed.
- vbus_on could disagree with the hardware. It now tracks the enable
  reference this driver holds; the regulator core keeps that reference
  when a disable fails, so the next update retries. VBUS found on at
  probe is switched off by taking and dropping a reference before the
  first state sync, and detach logs if the boost is still on.
- A late APCmdRes from a timed-out command could complete the next
  command's wait, and a repeated opcode such as CTRL1_W passes the echo
  check. After a timeout the command path waits 50 ms and re-arms the
  completion before it releases the mailbox.
- After a swap to source the enable can be refused with -EBUSY while the
  old source's VBUS decays. The enable is retried from vbus_work, which
  re-checks CCStat, up to 10 times at 100 ms.
- The probe-time poll kept UIC bits for the first sync, including the
  BOOT_POR that the probe's own hw_init answers, which cost a needless
  re-initialization. Those bits are dropped; the first sync reads levels.

